          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I feel love for you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving state toward Lear as true. The rest of her turn intensifies that avowal through comparisons claiming that her love exceeds what words or ordinary values can express.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The occurrence answers Lear's request for a comparative declaration of love; it does not create, renew, or affirm a specific commitment or obligation. Goneril's reference to what a child has loved compares the magnitude of her feeling rather than positioning herself as bound to future conduct. Lear's subsequent land grant is his response to the avowal, not evidence that her focal words themselves are a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The expression is a deliberate answer in a formal, solicited contest and is followed by an elaborate rhetorical expansion. Nothing represents it as a reflex-like affective discharge distinct from its assertive avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\\n\\nGONERIL.\\nSir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.89,\"reason\":\"No additional core force beyond the avowal is established. The declaration occurs within a competition tied to Lear's bounty and may serve a strategic purpose, but that intended effect is not by itself a separate illocutionary mode of the focal expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, explicitly connecting their answers to the distribution of his bounty. As the eldest and first respondent, Goneril addresses Lear with a direct avowal and then amplifies it through extravagant comparisons. Lear responds by granting her and Albany a portion of the kingdom.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no established undertaking: it avows exceptionally great filial love without specifying or affirming conduct to which Goneril is bound. Lear treats the speech as a successful answer and rewards Goneril with land, but this is uptake of her declaration in his love test rather than acceptance of a promise. The supplied evidence does not settle her sincerity.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognizable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The scene establishes the public, competitive setting and Lear's immediate response, but it does not independently settle whether Goneril actually feels the love she avows. No omitted or later events are used to infer sincerity, commitment, or eventual conduct.\"}"
